[Corollary 4 of arXiv:2608.19832, 'Sharp Convex Concentration for Symmetric Random Tensors with Subgaussian Coordinates'] There are absolute constants $K_0,c,C>0$ such that the following holds.
Let $0<s\le c\sqrt n$ and assume
$\mathcal I_{n,d}(s)\ge1$. Then there are independent centered,
variance-one coordinates with $\psi_2$ norm at most $K_0$ and a convex
one-Lipschitz function $f$ such that, for every $m\in\R$,
\begin{equation}
 \Pp\left\{
 \abs{f(X^{\otimes d})-m}
 \ge c n^{(d-1)/2}s
 \right\}
 \ge\exp\big(-C\mathcal I_{n,d}(s)\big).
\end{equation}

[Def 43 (Backbone exploration) of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $x,y\in V_ g$. Let $
\in \Omega_{G_{ g}}$ with $\sn=\{x,y\}$. The backbone of $
$, denoted by $\Gamma(
)$, is the unique oriented and edge self-avoiding path from $x$ to $y$, supported on edges $uv$ with $
_{uv}$ odd, and which is minimal for $\prec$. The backbone $\Gamma(
)=\{x_ix_{i+1}:  0\leq i < k\}$ is obtained via the following procedure:
 egin{enumerate}
    \item[$(i)$] Set $x_0=x$. The first edge $x_0x_1$ of $\Gamma(
)$ is the earliest of all the edges $e$ emerging from $x_0$ for which $
_e$ is odd.
    All edges $e$ containing $x_0$ and satisfying $e\prec x_0x_1$ and $e
eq x_0x_1$ are called \emph{explored} and have $
_e$ even.
    \item[$(ii)$] Each edge $x_ix_{i+1}$ with $i\ge 1$ is the earliest of all edges $e$ emerging from $x_i$ that have not been explored previously, and for which $
_e$ is odd.
    \item[$(iii)$] The exploration stops when it reaches $y=x_k$ for the first time.
\end{enumerate}
Let $\overline{\Gamma}(
)$ denote the set of explored edges, i.e. $\Gamma(
)$ together with all explored even edges.

[Lem 6 of arXiv:2607.24561, 'Range convergence and sharp one-arm asymptotics\\for the critical percolation cluster in h'] Let $\Lambda>3$. There exist positive constants $c_{\Lambda}$ and $c$, where $c_{\Lambda}$ depends only on $\Lambda$ (and $d$), such that
    \begin{align*}
        c_{\Lambda}r^{-2}\le \bP[0 \leftrightarrow Q_{z,r}]\le cr^{-2},\qquad \forall z\in Q_{0,\Lambda r}\setminus Q_{0,3r}.
    \end{align*}

