[Lemma extend-nat-trafo of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $n\ge0$, $\textbf{\textup{I}}_1,\dots,\textbf{\textup{I}}_n$ be topologically enriched categories, and let $X_i\in\textbf{\textup{Fun}}_\textup{cont}(\textbf{\textup{I}}_i,\textbf{\textup{Top}})$ for $i=1,\dots,n$ be arbitrary. Moreover, let $\textbf{\textup{D}}$ be any cocomplete category enriched and tensored over $\textbf{\textup{Top}}$, and let
    \[F,G\colon\prod_{i=1}^n\textbf{\textup{Fun}}_\textup{cont}(\textbf{\textup{I}}_i,\textbf{\textup{Top}})_{/X_i}\rightrightarrows\textbf{\textup{D}}\]
    be topological functors that preserve colimits and tensors in each variable. Then any natural transformation $\sigma$ between the restrictions $\prod_{i=1}^n(\textbf{\textup{y}}_{/X_i})^{\textup{op}}\rightrightarrows\textbf{\textup{D}}$ of $F$ and $G$ admits a unique extension to a natural transformation $F\to G$.
    \begin{proof}
        We will give the proof for $n=1$; the general case then follows inductively by adjoining over one of the factors.

        Given $Y\colon\textbf{\textup{I}}\to\textbf{\textup{Top}}$, we have for all $i,j\in\textbf{\textup{I}}$ maps 
        \begin{align*}
            \alpha_{i,j}\colon\textbf{\textup{maps}}(j,-)\times\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,j)\times Y(i)&\to\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,-)\times Y(i)\\
            \beta_{i,j}\colon\textbf{\textup{maps}}(j,-)\times\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,j)\times Y(i)&\to\textbf{\textup{maps}}(j,-)\times Y(j)
        \end{align*}
        via precomposition and the functoriality of $Y$, respectively. Moreover, we have for every $i\in \textbf{\textup{I}}$ a unique map $\gamma_i\colon\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,-)\times Y(i)\to Y$ such that $\gamma_i(\textup{id}_i,-)=\textup{id}$. These then assemble into a diagram
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=1.5em]
                \coprod\limits_{i,j\in I} \textbf{\textup{maps}}(j,-)\times\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,j)\times Y(i)\arrow[r, yshift=2pt,"\alpha"]\arrow[r,yshift=-2pt,"\beta"']& \coprod\limits_{i\in I} \textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,-)\times Y(i) \arrow[r,"\gamma"] & Y\rlap,
            \end{tikzcd}
        \end{equation}
        in ${\textup{Fun}}_\textup{cont}(\textbf{\textup{I}},\textbf{\textup{Top}})$ and the enriched Yoneda lemma says that this is a coequalizer diagram. Given any map $f\colon Y\to X$, we can then uniquely lift $((eq:coyoneda of the paper))$ to a diagram in ${\textup{Fun}}_\textup{cont}(\textbf{\textup{I}},\textbf{\textup{Top}})_{/X}$ expressing $f$ as a coequalizer. As $F$ and $G$ are assumed to preserve tensors, this then similarly expresses $F(Y)$ and $G(Y)$ as coequalizers. As any  transformation $F\to G$ is automatically enriched and hence preserves tensors, this shows that in any extension of $\sigma$ to a natural transformation $\bar\sigma\colon F\to G$, the map $\bar \sigma_Y\colon F(Y\to X)\to G(Y\to X)$ must be the map induced on coequalizers by
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=1.1em,cramped]
                \arrow[d,"{\coprod_{i,j}\eta\otimes (\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,j)\times Y(i))}"']\coprod\limits_{i,j\in I} F(\textbf{\textup{maps}}(j,-)\to X)\otimes (\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,j)\times Y(i))\arrow[r, yshift=2pt]\arrow[r,yshift=-2pt]& \coprod\limits_{i\in I} F(\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,-)\to X)\otimes Y(i)\arrow[d,"\coprod_{i}\eta\otimes Y(i)"]\\
                \coprod\limits_{i,j\in I} G(\textbf{\textup{maps}}(j,-)\to X)\otimes (\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,j)\times Y(i))\arrow[r, yshift=2pt]\arrow[r,yshift=-2pt]& \coprod\limits_{i\in I} G(\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,-)\to X)\otimes Y(i)\rlap.
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        In summary, we have shown that there exists for every $Y$ a unique map $\bar\sigma$ making each of the diagrams
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{tikzcd}
                F(\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,-)\to X)\otimes Y(i)\arrow[d,"\sigma"']\arrow[r]& F(Y)\arrow[d,"\bar\sigma"]\\
                G(\textbf{\textup{maps}}(i,-)\to X)\otimes Y(i)\arrow[r]& G(Y)
            \end{tikzcd}
        \end{equation}
        co (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem SouvlakiA of arXiv:2412.09290, 'Asymptotics of Harish-Chandra transform and infinitesimal freeness'] Let $A=A(N)$ be a random Hermitian matrix of size $N$ and assume
  that for every finite $r$ one has
  \begin{equation}
    \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{N} \log \text{} \mathbb{E} [H \text{}
    C (x_1, \ldots, x_r, 0^{N - r} ; \lambda_1 (A), \ldots, \lambda_N (A))] =
    \sum_{i = 1}^r \Psi (x_i), 
  \end{equation}
  where $\Psi$ is a smooth function in a complex neighborhood of $0$ and the above
  convergence is uniform in a complex neighborhood of $0^r$. Then the random measure
  $N^{- 1} \sum_{i = 1}^N \delta \left( N^{- 1} \lambda_i (A) \right)$ converges, as $N
  \rightarrow \infty$, in probability, in the sense of moments to a
  deterministic measure $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mu}}$ on $\mathbb{R}$ whose moments are
  given by
  \begin{equation}
    \int_{\mathbb{R}} t^k \ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\mu}} (d \text{} t) = \sum_{m = 0}^k
    \frac{k!}{m! (m + 1) ! (k - m) !}  \left. \frac{\mathrm{d}^m}{\mathrm{d} x^m}
    ((\Psi' (x))^{k - m}) \right|_{x = 0} . 
  \end{equation}

