[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2607.18186, 'Small Counterexamples to the Gaussian Moments Conjecture'] For every $A\in\C[z]$ and $m\geq1$,
\begin{equation}
  \E\bigl(A(Z)P_3^m\bigr)
  =m!\,[z^m]A(z)(1+z)^{m-1}.
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
  \E(P_3^m)=0,
  \qquad
  \E(Q_3P_3^m)=m!\neq0
  \qquad(m\geq1).
\end{equation}

[Lemme allomcond of arXiv:2603.21634, 'Individual-based stochastic model with unbounded growth, birth and death rates: a tightnes'] Under the allometric setting, we have
\begin{center}
$($Assumptions~(hyp:gainenergy of the paper) and (hyp:probamortel of the paper) $)\Leftrightarrow$ $(\delta \leq \alpha -1$, $\gamma = \alpha$ and $C_{\gamma} > C_{\alpha})$.
\end{center}

[Lemma lem:NBS of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] Suppose that $\mu$ is a {probability} {measure} on $\Omega \subseteq \mathbb{R}^N$ such
that for all {Lipschitz} continuous increasing functions
$f, g : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$ and $\mathbf{x} \sim \mu$ we have
\begin{equation}
    \operatorname{Cov}\mleft[ f(\mathbf{x}), g(\mathbf{x}) \mright] \geqslant 0.
\end{equation}
Then for all {Lipschitz continuous} functions $F, G : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$ (not necessarily increasing), we have
\begin{equation}
    \mleft| \operatorname{Cov} \mleft[ F(\mathbf{x}), G(\mathbf{x}) \mright] \mright|
    \leqslant \sum_{i,j=1}^N \operatorname{Lip}_i^\Omega(F) \operatorname{Lip}_j^\Omega(G) \operatorname{Cov}\mleft[ x^i, x^j \mright].
\end{equation}

