{"artifact_digest":"sha256:5eb2c410c297200516ab9d35668fca4f894982e4ad81c24f6cdf53a4d6173fd3","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/5e/5eb2c410c297200516ab9d35668fca4f894982e4ad81c24f6cdf53a4d6173fd3.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6b847409c543fec7d9cec98eb90305ebd5844c1e4f8a55f9be1ff68a44426255","bundle_entry_key":"2608-30579-theorem-2-conditional-fisher-information","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_129","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_129.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-30579-proposition-2-conditional-fisher-information","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $J_{n}=\mathbb{E}J(X_{n}\vert Y_{n}), V_{n}=\mathbb{E}\operatorname{Var}(X_{n}\vert Y_{n})$.  On the one hand,
  By the Cram\'{e}r--Rao inequality, for almost every $y_n$,
  \begin{equation}
        J(X_n\mid Y_n=y_n)\operatorname{Var}(X_n\mid Y_n=y_n)\geq 1.
  \end{equation}
Together with the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, this gives
\begin{equation}
        V_nJ_n
    \geq
    \left(
    \mathbb{E}\sqrt{
    J(X_n\mid Y_n)\operatorname{Var}(X_n\mid Y_n)
    }
    \right)^2
    \geq 1, \, \forall n\in\mathbb{N}^{+}.
