body:
[Definition MESSSAA of arXiv:2411.04590, 'Singular stochastic delay equations driven by fractional Brownian motion: Dynamics, longti'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$ be a measurable space. We call a family of Banach spaces $\{E_{\omega}\}_{\omega \in \Omega}$ a \emph{measurable field of Banach spaces} if there is a set of sections
			\begin{align*}
				\Delta \subset \prod_{\omega \in \Omega} E_{\omega}
			\end{align*}
			with the following properties:
			\begin{itemize}
				\item[(i)] $\Delta$ is a linear subspace of $\prod_{\omega \in \Omega} E_{\omega}$.
				\item[(ii)] There is a countable subset $\Delta_0 \subset \Delta$ such that for every $\omega \in \Omega$, the set $\{g(\omega)\, :\, g \in \Delta_0\}$ is dense in $E_{\omega}$.
				\item[(iii)] For every $g \in \Delta$, the map $\omega \mapsto \| g(\omega) \|_{E_{\omega}}$ is measurable.
			\end{itemize}
			\begin{remark}
				Alternatively, when \(\Omega\) is a metric space, we can define a stronger concept called \emph{continuous fields of Banach spaces}. In this case, the third condition in Definition (MESSSAA of the paper) must be replaced by:
				\begin{itemize}
					\item[(iii)$^{\prime}$] For every \(g \in \Delta\), the map \(\omega \mapsto \| g(\omega) \|_{E_{\omega}}\) is continuous.
				\end{itemize}
			\end{remark}

[Lemma lem:linear of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] For $\mathbf{W}_1, \mathbf{W}_2 \in \mathcal{W}_N$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \mu_N \mleft[ \widetilde{\mathbf{W}_1 \wedge \mathbf{W}_2} \mright] \cdot \mu_N \mleft[ \widetilde{\mathbf{W}_1 \vee \mathbf{W}_2} \mright]
    = \mu_N \mleft[\widetilde{\mathbf{W}}_1\mright] \cdot \mu_N \mleft[\widetilde{\mathbf{W}}_2\mright],
\end{equation}

