[Proposition 35 of arXiv:2510.24244, 'Statistical properties of Markov shifts: part II-LLT'] If all the spaces $\cX_j$ are  connected 
then for every $0<\del<T$ there are constants $c,C>0$ such that for all $n$,
\begin{equation}
\sup_{\del\leq |t|\leq T}\|\cL_{0,t}^n\|_{*}\leq Ce^{-c\sig_n}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, if $\sig_n\to \infty$ then $(f_j)$ is irreducible. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2608.29486, 'Cabled braids and their crossing matrices'] Let $b \in \mathcal{B}_n$. 
Let $b^{(k)}=f(b_1, b_2, \dots , b_n;b) \in \mathcal{B}_{n+1}$ with $b_1= \dots =b_{k-1}=b_{k+1}= \dots = b_n=id_1$ and $b_k=id_2$ ($k \in \{ 1, 2, \dots , n \}$). 
Then $C(b^{(k)})$ is obtained from $C(b)$ as follows. 
Divide $C(b)$ into $3 \times 3$ blocks by separating $k-1, 1, n-k$ rows and columns as follows: 
\begin{align*}
C(b)= \begin{bmatrix}
A_{11} & A_{12} & A_{13} \\
A_{21} & 0 & A_{23} \\
A_{31} & A_{32} & A_{33} 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{align*}
The crossing matrix of $b^{(k)}$ is given by 
\begin{align*}
C(b^{(k)})= \begin{bmatrix}
A_{11} & A_{12} & A_{12} & A_{13} \\
A_{21} & 0 & 0 & A_{23} \\
A_{21} & 0 & 0 & A_{23} \\
A_{31} & A_{32} & A_{32} & A_{33} 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Namely, $C(b^{(k)})$ is obtained from $C(b)$ by duplicating the $k^{th}$ row and column. 
When $k=1$ or $n$, this is obtained from a $2 \times 2$ block decomposition. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

