{\sf B}^{(2\mu)}(p) &\le {\sf B}^{(0)}(p_c) + \gamma .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

[Proposition bound for Airy function of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] [olver1997asymptotics] The Airy function $Ai(x)$ given in Definition (def4 classic DPPs of the paper) is the unique solution of the {Airy equation}: $y''(x)=xy(x)$ vanishing at $+\infty$. On $\mathbb{R}^+$, $Ai(x)$ is a decreasing function such that
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
        &0<Ai(x)\le\frac{1}{2\sqrt{\pi}x^{\frac{1}{4}}}\exp\left(-\frac{2}{3}x^\frac{3}{2}\right),\\&Ai(x)\sim\frac{1}{2\sqrt{\pi}x^{\frac{1}{4}}}\exp\left(-\frac{2}{3}x^\frac{3}{2}\right)~for~x~large.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
  Moreover, its derivative $Ai'(x)$, is an increasing negative function on $\mathbb{R}^+$ according to the Airy equation: $Ai''(x)=xAi(x)\ge0$ and hence, $|Ai'(x)|$ is a decreasing function such that
  \begin{equation}
      |Ai'(x)|\le \frac{x^\frac{1}{4}}{2\sqrt\pi}\exp\left(-\frac{2}{3}x^\frac{3}{2}\right).
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

