[Lemma 20 of arXiv:2609.02019, '2609.02019'] There exists a strictly positive continuous adapted process
		$\mathscr Z=(\mathscr Z(t))_{t\ge0}$ such that, almost surely on
		$\Omega_M$, the random variable $\mathscr Z(\tau^*)$ is finite and
		strictly positive and, for all $t<\tau^*$ sufficiently close to
		$\tau^*$,
		\begin{equation}
			Y(t)
			=
			\int_t^{\tau^*}
			\frac{\mathscr Z(r)}{\mathscr Z(t)}
			\left(\frac12+\eta(r)\right)\d r.
		\end{equation}
		Moreover,
		\begin{equation}
			\lim_{t\uparrow\tau^*}
			\sup_{r\in[t,\tau^*]}
			\left|
			\frac{\mathscr Z(r)}{\mathscr Z(t)}-1
			\right|
			=0.
		\end{equation}

