body:
[Lemma lem:uniform_Bound_diam of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] For any $\rho>0$ there exists $c>0$ such that
    \begin{align*}
\inf_D\mathbb P(\text{every loop of } \mathrm{CLE}_4(D) \text{ has diameter less than or equal to }  \rho) \geq c,    \end{align*}
where the infimum is taken over all open sets $D\subseteq \mathbb D$ such that $D$ is a countable union of pairwise disjoint simply connected domains. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop_pointtoset_harnack of arXiv:2412.05706, 'Quasi-multiplicativity and regularity for metric graph Gaussian free fields'] For any $d\ge 3$, there exists a constant $
	\refstepcounter{cnstcnt}
	\ensuremath{c_{\thecnstcnt}}(d)>0$ such that for any $N\ge 1$, $A\subset [\widetilde{B}(N)]^{c}$, $x_1,x_2\in B(\ensuremath{c_{\ref*{const_pointtoset_harnack}}}N^{1-\varsigma_d(N)})$, $M\le \frac{1}{2}(|x_1|\land |x_2|)$ and $D\subset \widetilde{B}(M)\cup [\widetilde{B}(N)]^{c}$,
		\begin{equation}
		\mathbb{P}^D\big( A \xleftrightarrow{\ge 0}  x_1 \big) \asymp \mathbb{P}^{D}\big( A \xleftrightarrow{\ge 0}  x_2 \big). 
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition WPD-NP-main of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] For any $N \in \left\{ {0,1, \cdots ,10} \right\} $, $n \in {\mathbb{Z}^ + } \cup \left\{ 0 \right\}$ and $t \in \mathrm{supp}_t\ w_{q + 1,n + 1}^{(n)}$, there exists a real number $\alpha \in \left( {\frac{\beta }{{b}},\beta } \right)$ such that the $\left( {n + 1} \right)$-step Newtonian perturbation $w_{q + 1,n + 1}^{(n)}\left( {t,x} \right)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {w_{q + 1,n + 1}^{(n)}} \right\|_N} \lesssim \mu _{q + 1}^{ - 1}{\delta _{q + 1}}\lambda _q^{N + 1}l_q^{ - \alpha }  ,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {{{\bar D}_{t,q}}w_{q + 1,n + 1}^{(n)}} \right\|_N} \lesssim {\delta _{q + 1}}\lambda _q^{N + 1}l_q^{ - \alpha }  .
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

