body:
[Definition defin:OUsolution of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] We say that a process $(\mathcal{Z}_t)_{t\in [0,T]}$ with continuous $\mathscr{S}'(\mathbb{R} )$-valued trajectories is a solution to the \emph{Ornstein-Uhlenbeck equation}
    \begin{equation}
    \partial_t\mathcal{Z}_t = \mathfrak{a}\Delta\mathcal{Z}_t +\sqrt{2\mathfrak{a}\sigma^2}\nabla\dot{\mathcal{W}}_t
    \end{equation}
    if it satisfies conditions (energysol_item1 of the paper) and (energysol_item3 of the paper) with $\mathfrak{g}=0$ in (defin:energysolution of the paper). The existence and uniqueness of solutions to the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck equation is a classical result, see [holley_generalized_1978].

[Thm lower alpha of arXiv:2603.15323, '\Large \bf Small-time heat decay for stable processes on fractal drums'] Let $G$ be an open set given as in (equation eqn:SS of the paper), $\alpha\in(0,d-\mathfrak{b}]$, and Condition (cond:lower alpha of the paper) is satisfied. Then, 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{t\to 0}\frac{m(G)-Q_{G}^{(\alpha)}(t)}{t}=\textup{Per}^{(\alpha)}(G)=\int_{G}\int_{G^c}\frac{c(\alpha, d)}{|x-y|^{d+\alpha}}dydx,
\end{equation}
where the constant $c(d,\alpha) $ is given by 
\begin{equation}
c(\alpha, d) = \frac{\alpha \Gamma\big(\frac{d +\alpha} 2\big)} {2^{1 - \alpha} \pi^{d/2}\Gamma\big(1 - \frac \alpha 2\big)}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

