[Theorem 8 (Refined \cref{thm:intro-static}) of arXiv:2608.31008, 'Stability of gravitational instantons with a bounded Killing vector field'] Let $(M^4,g)$ be connected, oriented and Ricci-flat, and let $K\not\equiv0$ be a
Killing field satisfying
\begin{equation}
 \kappa\wedge d\kappa=0.
\end{equation}
Put $F=d\kappa$, $F^\pm$ its $\pm$-selfdual parts, and $h=F^-\circ F^+$.  Then $h$ is TT and
\begin{equation}
 \langle\cE_gh,h\rangle
 =-\frac12\Big|d\Big(\tfrac{|F|^2}{2}\Big)\Big|^2.
\end{equation}
If the right-hand side vanishes identically, then $g$ is flat.

[Definition def:fringe of arXiv:2606.24465, 'History estimation in random recursive trees:\\Pointwise approach via iterated Jordan cent'] For a vertex $v\in [n]$, we call the subtree of $v$ in the rooted tree $(T_n, 1)$ the fringe tree attached to $v$, or the fringe tree of $v$. For its size, we write 
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{Fr}_n(v):=|(T_n, 1)_{v\downarrow}|=|(T_n, \mathrm{pa}(v))_{v\downarrow}|,
\end{equation}
    where the last equality holds for all $v \geq 2$.
    Furthermore, given $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $v \in [n]$, we define the fringe tree of $v$ witnessed after time $m$ as the set of vertices in $(T_n, 1)_{v\downarrow}$ whose unique path to $v$ in $T_n$ has no edges  that arrived before time $m+1$.  We write $\mathrm{Fr}_n^{>m}(v)$ for its size.

[Prop pr.PreKin of arXiv:2411.13099, 'Long time behavior of killed Feynman-Kac semigroups with singular Schr{ö}dinger potentials'] Then, for all $\mathsf y\in \mathscr R^{2d}$,   there exists a unique strong solution $(Y_t(\mathsf y),t\ge 0)$ to (equation eq.Lcin of the paper) over $\mathscr R^{2d}$ whose sample paths are a.s. continuous.  
Finally, for all $T>0$,   the following  Girsanov's formula holds for all $T>0$,  
\begin{equation}
\frac{d \mathbf P_{\mathsf y}}{d\mathbf P_{\mathsf y}^0}{  \Big|_{\mathcal F_T}}= \mathbf  m^0_T(\mathsf y)  ,
\end{equation}
where $ \mathbf P_{\mathsf y}$ (resp. $ \mathbf P^0_{\mathsf y}$)  is the law of  $(Y_t(\mathsf y),t\ge 0)$ (resp. of $(Y_t^0(\mathsf y),t\ge 0)$, see (equation eq.Lcin0 of the paper)), and $(\mathbf m_t^0,t\ge 0)$ is the   Doléans-Dade exponential (true) martingale defined by $\mathbf m_t^0=\exp\big[  - \int_0^t   \big(\gamma v_s^0+\nabla \mathbf V_{\mathbf c}(x_s^0)\big)dB_s -\frac 12 \int_0^t  \big\vert   \gamma v_s^0+\!\nabla \mathbf V_{\mathbf c}(x_s^0)\big\vert^2 ds   \big]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition Coordinate reflections of arXiv:2605.16162, 'Deconfinement For $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ Lattice Yang--Mills at Strong Coupling'] For any coordinate hyperplane in $\Z^d$ of the form $P=\{(x_1,\dots,x_d) \in \R^d:x_i=a\}$ for $i \in [d]$ and $a \in \Z \cup \frac{1}{2}\Z$, we let $\Theta_P:E_{\Lambda} \to E_{\Lambda}$ denote the map which reflects edges over $P$ (keeping track of orientation so that edges parallel to the hyperplane reverse orientation after reflection). Moreover for any configuration $Q=(Q_e)_{e \in E_{\Lambda}^+} \in G^{E_{\Lambda}^+}$
we abuse notation by defining the reflected configuration $\Theta_P Q$ by $(\Theta_P Q)_e :=Q_{\Theta_p e} $, and lastly for any local observable $f: G^{E_{\Lambda}}\to \C$ we let
\begin{equs}
    (\Theta_P f)(Q):=f(\Theta_P Q).
\end{equs}

[Definition 2 (Ensembles with perturbed inputs) of arXiv:2609.01397, 'Measuring consistency via ensemble margin and local prediction variability: Auditing decis'] For a fixed $x$, Rashomon \smallset{} set $\retailset$, and noise realizations $\localtest_j$, for each $j$ we define the ensemble value at $\localtest_j$ by
    \begin{align}
    \ensmretset(\retailset,\localtest_j) 
    =  \frac{1}{\Nretail}\sum_{k=1}^{\Nretail} f_{k}(\localtest_j).
    \end{align}
Averaging over the $\nogaussianpts$ noise realizations, we have
     \begin{align}
    \ensmretset(\retailset,\localtestmat) 
    = \frac{1}{\Nretail} \sum_{k=1}^{\Nretail}
    \frac{1}{\nogaussianpts}\sum_{j=1}^{\nogaussianpts}f_{k}(\localtest_{j}).
    \end{align}

[Proposition B4z of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] For $N$ large enough, we can find an $s_1>0$ such that for any $s$ with $s_1\le s\le \frac{2\tilde{\mu}_{n,N}}{3\tilde{\sigma}_{N,n}}$ and $z$ around $z_1$,
      \begin{equation*}
          \frac{2\kappa_N}{3}\zeta^\frac{3}{2}(z),~~\frac{2\kappa_{n-1,N}}{3}\zeta^\frac{3}{2}\left(\frac{\tilde{x}_N(s)}{\kappa_{n-1,N}}\right)~~and~~\frac{2\kappa_{n,N-1}}{3}\zeta^\frac{3}{2}\left(\frac{\tilde{x}_N(s)}{\kappa_{n,N-1}}\right)\ge s
      \end{equation*}
      where $\tilde{x}_N(s)=\tilde{\mu}_{n,N}-\tilde{\sigma}_{n,N}s$ with respect to $\tilde{\mu}_{n,N}$ and $\tilde{\sigma}_{n,N}$ restricted as (equation restriction of recaling of the paper). In particular, the inequalities hold for $s_1\le s\le \frac{e_0\tilde{\mu}_{n,N}}{\tilde{\sigma}_{N,n}}$ since $e_0<\frac{2}{3}$.

