body:
[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2606.21111, 'On Elephant Random Walk with Delayed Amnesia'] Let $\{M_n, \mathcal{F}_n\}_{n\ge1}$ be a locally square-integrable martingale in
$\mathbb{R}^{d}$ with predictable
quadratic variation $\{\langle M \rangle_n\}_{n\ge1}$.
Also, let $\{V_n\}_{n\ge1}$ be a sequence of non-random square matrices of order $d$ such that $\|V_n\|$ decreases to $0$ as $n\to \infty$. Assume that there exists a symmetric and positive semi-definite matrix $V$ such that
\begin{equation}
V_n \langle M \rangle_n V_n^t
\xrightarrow{p} V.
\end{equation}
Moreover, assume that Lindeberg's condition is satisfied, that is, for all $\varepsilon>0$,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{k=1}^{n}
\mathbb{E}(
\|V_n \Delta M_k\|^{2}
\mathbb{I}_{\{\|V_n\Delta M_k\|>\varepsilon\}}| \mathcal{F}_{k-1})
\xrightarrow{p} 0,
\end{equation}
where $\Delta M_n=M_n-M_{n-1}$, $M_0=0$. Then, $V_n M_n
\xrightarrow{d}
\mathcal{N}(0,V)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Cor corSmallCTnm of arXiv:2411.18563, 'Lower tails for triangles inside the critical window'] Fix  $b>0$ satisfying $ b^2 < W(2/e)$. If  $m = (1+o(1)) \frac{b}{2} n^{3/2}$, then
 \begin{equation}

      \lim_{n \to \infty}   \frac{1}{n^{3/2}} \log \mathbb{P}_m(X=0) = -\frac{ b^3}{6} \,.
    \end{equation}
  In other words, 
    \begin{equation}
        T(n,m) = \binom{\binom{n}{2}}{m} \exp \left( - \frac{4}{3}\left( \frac{m}{n} \right)^3 + o(n^{3/2})\right).
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:equiv_weak of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $v:(0,T)\to L^{1}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, and let also $\sigma$, $b$ be as in (equation eq:sig_b_deg_den of the paper) and $\nu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. If either
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] Assumption (assum:b_0 of the paper)-(b) is satisfied,
\item[(ii)] or, Assumption (assum:b_0 of the paper)-(b-loc) is satisfied and $v\in L^{\infty}_{T}L^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^d)$,
 \end{itemize}
then  $v$ is a \emph{pseudo-mild solution} to (equation eq:nl_fp_cp_den_kin of the paper) if and only if it is a weak solution to D-NL-FP$(b,\sigma; \nu)$ (see Definition (def:sol_nl_fp_den of the paper)). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:weighted_entropy_tail of arXiv:2605.15401, 'Noise Tradeoffs, Stationary Information Flow, and Structural Balance in Unit-Birth Network'] Let $\pi$ be a probability distribution on $S=\mathbb{Z}_{\ge0}^N$ with full support satisfying
$\mathbb{E}_\pi[s(X)^2]<\infty$, where $s(x)=\|x\|_1$. Then
\begin{equation}
\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)\,(1+s(x))\,|\log\pi(x)|<\infty.
\end{equation}
Moreover, if $\Lambda:S\to[0,\infty)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}

\Lambda(x)\le C_\Lambda(1+s(x))\qquad \forall x\in S,
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)\,\Lambda(x)\,\bigl(1+|\log\pi(x)|\bigr)<\infty.
\end{equation}

[Proposition 24 (Compact reduction and path-law pushforward) of arXiv:2609.02196, 'Schr\"odinger Bridges on Lie Group Manifolds for Probabilistic Intrinsic Generation'] Under the reduced-representation assumption in Appendix~\ref{app:reduction-details}, the stochastic learning problem is an RCCBM problem on \(\G_{\rm red}\). If the reconstruction is a \(C^2\)-diffeomorphism onto the modeled conformation manifold, the induced path map preserves relative entropy,
\begin{equation}
\KL(P_{\rm red}^{\rm SB}\|\widehat P_{\rm red})
=\KL((\mathbf\Phi_{\rm red})_\#P_{\rm red}^{\rm SB}
\|(\mathbf\Phi_{\rm red})_\#\widehat P_{\rm red}).
\end{equation}
Without injectivity, only the data-processing inequality
\begin{equation}
\KL((\mathbf{\mathcal R}_{\rm red})_\#P\|
(\mathbf{\mathcal R}_{\rm red})_\#Q)\leq\KL(P\|Q)
\end{equation}
is available. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm 10 of arXiv:2609.00598, 'Supercritical mean--field limit for magnetized Hamiltonian dynamics'] Let $f^{\mathrm{in}}\in W^{1,\infty}(\mathbb{T}^{2}\times \mathbb{R}^{2})$
and satisfy
\begin{align*}
\int_{\mathbb{T}^{2}\times \mathbb{R}^{2}}\left\vert \xi\right\vert^{k} f^{\mathrm{in}} (x,\xi) \ \dd x\dd \xi<\infty\qquad\mbox{for some}\ k\ \mbox{sufficiently large}.  
\end{align*}
Then there exists a unique solution $f\in W^{1,\infty}([0,T]\times \mathbb{T}^{2}\times \mathbb{R}^{2})$ to \eqref{magnetized vp} with initial data $f^{\mathrm{in}}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary cor:minimum of arXiv:2609.03858, 'High-Dimensional Learning Dynamics of \\ Attention-Indexed Models'] Let $\mathcal{K} \subseteq \{1, 2, \dots, K\}$ denote the index set of the learnable weights, while the remaining weights $\{S^k_{ij}\}_{k \notin \mathcal{K}}$ are fixed and satisfy the condition $\lim_{d\to\infty} \|S^k_{ij}\|_{\text{op}} / \|S^k_{ij}\|_F = 0$. Define the order parameter set $Q$ as
\begin{equation}
Q:=\left\{\left\{\frac{1}{\sqrt{d}}{\rm Tr}[S^k_{ij}]\right\}_{i,j,k},\left\{\frac{1}{d}{\rm Tr}[S^k_{ij}S^{k'}_{i'j'}], \frac{1}{d}{\rm Tr}[S^k_{ij}(S^{k'}_{i'j'})^T]\right\}_{i,j,k,i',j',k'}\right\}.
\end{equation}
Let $\mathcal{Q}$ denote the feasible domain of $Q$ realizable by matrix sequences satisfying Assumption (assum:attention-index of the paper) in the limit $d \to \infty$, subject to the constraints imposed by the fixed weights $\{S^k_{ij}\}_{k \notin \mathcal{K}}$. Let $\mathcal{R}(Q) := \mathbb{E}_{G}[\mathcal{L}(\{G_{ijk}\}_{i,j,k=1}^{L,L,K})]$ denote the expected population loss in Theorem (theo:general of the paper) specified by $Q$ and the covariance $\mathcal{C}$.

Under the regularity assumption specified in Appendix (app:proof-cor-minimum of the paper),
\begin{equation}
\lim_{d\to\infty} \inf_{\{S^k_{ij}\}_{k\in\mathcal{K}}} \mathbb{E}_x\left[\mathcal{L}\left(\left\{\frac{x_i^TS^k_{ij}x_j}{\sqrt{d}}\right\}_{i,j,k=1}^{L,L,K}\right)\right] = \inf_{Q \in \mathcal{Q}} \mathcal{R}(Q).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem: property for di in adding external field of arXiv:2606.13147, 'Scaling limits of the single-curve interface and outermost loops in the planar random fiel'] Under the assumptions in Proposition~(prop: adding external field fluctuation of the paper), $d_i$ (recalling (equation eq: def of di in adding external field of the paper)) satisfies condition (item: di of the paper), i.e., \begin{align}

        {\mathbb{E}} \sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathsf{d}_i^2&\le c_4{\epsilon}^4M^{\frac{7}{2}}\cdot \iota^{-\frac{7}{4}-\kappa_{1}}.
    \end{align}In addition, we have \begin{equation}
        {\mathbb{P}}\Big(|\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathsf{d}_i|\ge \sqrt{ \iota^{-2\kappa_{1}}\cdot \sum_{i=1}^{n}\sigma_i^2}\Big)\le c_4\iota^{\kappa_{1}}.
    \end{equation}

