{"artifact_digest":"sha256:d8bd04aeca75f3e3e90932c4361059b6b31cc8226228df518142c48dc8f809e1","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/d8/d8bd04aeca75f3e3e90932c4361059b6b31cc8226228df518142c48dc8f809e1.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6d2fc941644ea462cd50f312c4f1e40f3de6755cabcff34ef55df5a99c09d084","bundle_entry_key":"single-edge-partition-graph-matrix-sharp-norm","bundle_id":"population.w344-open-problem-p0018-checked-partials-20261010","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"w344/bundle_009.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:probability.graph_matrices:single-edge-partition-graph-matrix-sharp-norm","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Randomstrasse101, arXiv:2603.29571, Definition 14.2 and Conjecture 31, PDF p.14, define the cited restricted graph matrices using a partition into left and right vertex sets. Overlapping index vertices and unindexed internal vertices are not supplied by that displayed definition. The diagonal signed-degree experiment in special_case_measurements.jsonl therefore belongs to a broader convention and is explicitly excluded as a special case of this literal problem. Its finite calculations are retained only as a scope warning.

A valid shape has two distinct vertices u,v, one edge uv, U={u} and V={v}. Injective labeling gives an n by n symmetric Rademacher matrix A with zero diagonal. It satisfies E||A||=Theta(sqrt(n)), so f=1/2 and g=0 for this shape. The lower bound is deterministic: ||A||>=||A||_F/sqrt(n)=sqrt(n-1). For a fixed unit real x, x^T A x=sum_(i<j)2x_i x_j epsilon_ij. Its moment-generating function is at most exp(s^2), since cosh z<=exp(z^2/2) and sum_(i<j)x_i^2 x_j^2<=1/2. Hence P(|x^T A x|>=r)<=2 exp(-r^2/4). A 1/4 net of the unit sphere has at most 9^n points and ||A||<=2 max_net |x^T A x|; a union bound and integration of this tail give E||A||<=C sqrt(n). This proves the shape exponents, without resolving the all-shapes existence conjecture.

valid_shape_measurements.json uses 12 samples each at n=64,128,256 and verifies the shape's n(n-1) nonzero entries. The mean norm divided by sqrt(n) is 1.926,1.955,1.974. At n=256 the old Frobenius upper bound is 255.500 and the mean norm is 31.5894, a factor 8.088 numerical gap. The experiment illustrates the bound's loss; the proved improvement is the order n to order sqrt(n) for this one shape.","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:82f2e1e16932e0d5f42bf54d56baf9c3a33e2111921a29dabe57ff03d59900f9","revision_digest":"sha256:c31e18510c0b2b56764891bba3785f18638d4ceab8ead33f5700c14af007c2ea","source_id":"owner-w344-open-problems-20261010:single-edge-partition-graph-matrix-sharp-norm","source_revision":"2026-10-10 worker result; proof; independent audit pending w346; novelty unverified","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

