	Note that, if $\alpha(s_0)=\tau(s_0)=0$, then $\eta(s_0)=\pm1$ and  $\psi(s)=(0,0,\pm1)$, that is, a trivial solution. Let $\alpha(s_0)>0$, thus $s_{0}$ is a local minimum point of $\alpha(s)$. If there is another critical point $s_1$ of $\alpha(s)$ such that $s_0$ and $s_1$ are consecutive, then $\alpha(s_1)>\alpha(s_0)>0$, because $s_0$ is  a local minimum point. Thus, $\alpha^{\prime \prime}(s_1)=\alpha(s_1)>0$ and $s_1$ is a local minimum point $\alpha(s)$, this is absurd. ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:c28457f3781b409774f5ff673ac29fbd058e531884afb7f786ed64a5753f0588","revision_digest":"sha256:c957da88e31f2ae7ca6718414cd80f80731a11ce80c54d61d2c292637001e760","source_id":"arxiv:2608.27093","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

