[Definition def.finite-volHamiltonian of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Let $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}_n$ be a finite set, and let $(J_{xy})_{ \{ x, y \} \in E(\Lambda)} \in [0, \infty)^{E(\Lambda)} $ be a collection of non-negative conductances. We define the Wells' disorder to be the probability distribution on the space of site percolation configurations $\{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda \cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}$ given by the identity: for any $r \in \{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda \cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}$
    \begin{equation} 
        \nu_{\Lambda, J} (\{ r \}) := \frac{Z_{\Lambda, J,r}^{\mathrm{XY}}}{\bar Z_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}}} ~~\mbox{where} ~~ \bar Z_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}} = \sum_{ r \in \{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda\cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}} Z_{\Lambda, J,r}^{\mathrm{XY}}.
    \end{equation}
When the set $\Lambda$ is the box $\Lambda_L^n,$ we denote this measure by $\nu_{L, J}$.

