{"artifact_digest":"sha256:6b54b3d8b5ced47e11decd828062bd839f140f933d8be770437d1e6355969a58","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/6b/6b54b3d8b5ced47e11decd828062bd839f140f933d8be770437d1e6355969a58.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:21681429c98de93dc57b2a8d9d0d067a14d225ce1d1823b5312c6b1ec87804f8","bundle_entry_key":"2512-17747-theorem-25-exponential-moments-of-the-wid","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_096","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_096.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2512-17747-corollary-25-exponential-moments-of-the-wid","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We start by observing that $B^{(n)}_{x,y}$ can be mapped to $B^{(n)}_{y-x}$ a bridge from $0$ to $y-x$, by simply translating the walk.
    Moreover, shifting a walk does not affect its width, so we have
    \begin{align*}
        w\big(B^{(n)}_{x,y}\big)\overset{d}{=}w\big(B^{(n)}_{y-x}\big)\,.
    \end{align*}
    Further using that $0<w(B^{(n)}_{y-x})\leq n$, we see that the desired expected value re-writes as
    \begin{align*}
