{"artifact_digest":"sha256:6e89e31547dcb460291ef3e57ae62cc81fa77cd7fbac67a1a1b0ead942bbf7c3","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/6e/6e89e31547dcb460291ef3e57ae62cc81fa77cd7fbac67a1a1b0ead942bbf7c3.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6479ee448bcdabc2742df4bc89848f35edc2885deed906f4695c60eddf861bd6","bundle_entry_key":"2509-01797-lemma-43-lem-bound-ed","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_007","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s0_007.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2509-01797-lemma-43-lem-bound-ed","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Since both probabilities in \eqref{Eq bound ED} are conformally invariant,
we may replace the domain $D$ by the unit disk $\D$,
the point $z$ by $0$, and $w$ by a point $r\in [0,1)$
such that
\begin{displaymath}
G_{\D}(0,r) = G_{D}(z,w).
\end{displaymath}
Since
\begin{displaymath}
G_{\D}(0,r) = \dfrac{1}{2\pi} \log r^{-1},
\end{displaymath}
we get that
\begin{displaymath}
