{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1cb6628807c5b45583652820353bea98126471ba30ac33dc89723f4a3794a3d8","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/1c/1cb6628807c5b45583652820353bea98126471ba30ac33dc89723f4a3794a3d8.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:d8a16b5dbeb912afc9b84ab14da2c1f4fcb29b86a6bf502d112c78707544ec25","bundle_entry_key":"2609-00233-theorem-95-cite-hatcher-wahl-mcg3manifold","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_119","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_119.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-00233-theorem-95-cite-hatcher-wahl-mcg3manifold","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Hatcher--Wahl [Hatcher_Wahl_MCG3Manifold] describe how to extract (ii) from the literature. The necessity and sufficiency of the remaining three diffeomorphisms is from Hong--McCullough [HongMcCullough2013].
\begin{itemize}
    \item The statement for reducible $M$ follows from the statement for irreducible $M$ together with a result of McCullough [McCulloughTopAlgAuts3mfds90]. An alternative proof can be found in [Hatcher_Wahl_MCG3Manifold]. Note that for irreducible $M$ with (possibly empty) incompressible boundary, the statement says that $\rho$ is injective.
    \item Waldhausen [Waldhausen1968] shows the result for Haken manifolds that are closed or have incompressible boundary ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:52e5d086f644fb68eddc312e5ab746b2bcf2c603584226357f188f277c09c243","revision_digest":"sha256:d4cc7bc91f4b59fd25880f7be721e900d97c43985314439c9abed6d9a7430351","source_id":"arxiv:2609.00233","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

body:
[Proposition prop:non-cros of arXiv:2606.12143, 'Continuous stochastic flows driven by white noise and their duals'] Suppose that $(\sigma,b)$ satisfy Assumption~(assu:H of the paper) and conditions~(item:1a of the paper)--(item:1d of the paper). Then, almost surely, for all $(a_1,r_1),(a_2,r_2)\in \mathbb D^2$ and all $y,z\in {\mathbb R}$,
    \begin{align*}
        \overline\mathcal S_{r_1,y}(a_1)<  \overline\mathcal S_{r_2,y}(a_2) \ \Longrightarrow \ \overline\mathcal S_{r_1,z}(a_1)\le \overline\mathcal S_{r_2,z}(a_2).
    \end{align*}

[Lemma lem_large_dev_up of arXiv:2604.14851, 'Pool model: a mass preserving multi particle aggregation process'] For all fixed $\lambda>0$ and any $\epsilon>0$, there is an event $E_{\epsilon}$ such that ${\bf P}(E_{\epsilon})=1$ and that for all $\omega\in E_{\epsilon}$ there is some $R_0(\omega)<\infty$ so that 
		 \begin{equation} 

		|A_0(\omega)\cap B(R)|<(\lambda+\epsilon) \pi R^2, \ \forall R\ge R_0(\omega).
		 \end{equation}

[Corollary cor:tail-rate of arXiv:2607.04620, 'The Exact Ville Identity\\ \large From the Absorbing Case to the General Law\\ with an App'] Let $S_n$ be a centered martingale with $\mathbb{E}[S_n^2]=n$, and let $T$ be a first-passage time with $L=\mathbb{E}[S_T\mathbf{1}(T<\infty)]$ finite as in (thm:conservation of the paper). Then, from the moment inequalities of [klass2026beyond], for every $p>1$,
\begin{equation}
  |L|^{p/(p-1)}
  \le
  \liminf_{n\to\infty}\mathbb{P}(T>n)
  \left(\mathbb{E}[|S_n|^p\mathbf{1}(T>n)]\right)^{1/(p-1)}.

\end{equation}
The case $p=2$, after dropping the indicator inside the moment, gives the cruder $\liminf_n n\mathbb{P}(T>n)\ge L^2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 14 (Relative Palm determinant comparison) of arXiv:2608.20101, '\bfseries Largest bulk gap of the complex Ginibre ensemble'] Fix \(s<1\) and \(C_0<\infty\). There are \(c,C>0\) such that, uniformly for
\(|a|\le s\) and \(0\le r\le C_0n^{-1/2}(\log n)^{1/4}\),
\begin{equation}
 0\le\log\frac{q_n(a,r)}{q(\sqrt n\,r)}\le Ce^{-cn+nr^2}.
\end{equation}
In particular, \(q_n(a,r)/q(\sqrt n\,r)=1+O(e^{-c'n})\) uniformly in this range.
Also
\begin{equation}
 K_n(a,a)=\frac n\pi\bigl(1+O(e^{-c'n})\bigr).
\end{equation}

[Lemma 17 (Resolvent representation of hitting) of arXiv:2607.13210, 'Punctured Wilson--Evolving Sets and Root Identities for Massive Kirchhoff Forests'] For $x,y$ in $V$, let
\begin{equation*}
G_q(x,y)
  :=
  \int_0^\infty e^{-qt}P_t(x,y)\dd t.
\end{equation*}
Then, we find
\begin{equation}
  h_q(x,y)
  =
  \frac{G_q(x,y)}{G_q(y,y)}.
\end{equation}

[Lemma notation:LT of arXiv:2511.15893, 'On a Class of Dynamical Poisson-Voronoi Tessellations'] For any $\gamma\geq 0$, the Laplace transform of $H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}^2$, under the Palm probability distribution $\mathbb{P}^0_{{\mathcal A}_{\scriptscriptstyle V}}$ of ${\mathcal A}_{\scriptscriptstyle V}$, is given by 

\begin{equation}
    {\mathcal L }^0_{H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}^2}(\gamma)= \left(1+\frac{\gamma}{\lambda\pi}\right)^{-1/2}, 
\end{equation}

that is, $H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}$  follows a Nakagami distribution with parameter $\left({\frac{1}{2}}, \frac{1}{2\lambda\pi}\right)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem_hops of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] Let~$\gamma = (x_0,\ldots, x_n)$ be a path in~$\mathcal G^t$. Assume that~$x_0$ and~$x_n$ belong to~$\mathcal H$ (the infinite cluster of~$\mathcal G$), and~$x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1}$ do not belong to~$\mathcal H$ (so that each of them is either in~$t\Z^d$ or in some finite cluster of~$\mathcal G$). Let
	\[b:= \max\left\{\|y-x\|_\infty:\; \begin{array}{l} x, y \in V,\; x \text{ and } y \text{ are in the same finite} \\ \text{cluster of }\mathcal G, \text{ and this cluster is visited by } \gamma \end{array}\right\},\]
		that is,~$b$ is the maximum~$\ell_\infty$-diameter of all finite clusters of~$\mathcal G$ intersected by~$\gamma$. Then,
	\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{e \in \gamma} \tau^t_e \ge \frac{\K t}{2t+b}\cdot \|x_n -x_0\|_\infty.
	\end{equation*}

