{"artifact_digest":"sha256:dcd9cd675328d6b871b079e4a506985b9045f3dce154756bba7a8d56a30ccd5a","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/dc/dcd9cd675328d6b871b079e4a506985b9045f3dce154756bba7a8d56a30ccd5a.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:eb18fa18d774709c2015f01d87ee0ca275c2d09f2bbc70a839e4b6cb4cceb5d3","bundle_entry_key":"2605-08844-theorem-26-corollary-26","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_028","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_028.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2605-08844-corollary-26-corollary-26","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The proof of the right inequality immediately from the definition of $NC(\ell)_n$
\begin{align*}
NC(\ell)_n
&\leq d^n \sum_{i=0}^{\lfloor (t+n)/2 \rfloor}
\binom{t}{i} d^{\,t-i} \\
&= d^n \sum_{i=0}^{\min\left(t, \lfloor (t+n)/2 \rfloor\right)}
\binom{t}{i} d^{\,t-i} \\
&\leq d^n \sum_{i=0}^{t}
\binom{t}{i} d^{\,t-i} \\
&\leq d^n (1+d)^t.
\end{align*}
For the left inequality, define the binomially distributed random variable $V \sim B(t, \frac{1}{d+1})$. Then
\begin{align*}
    |NC(\ell)_n| &= (d+1)^t\left( d^n \mathbb{P}(V \leq \floor{(n+t)/2}) - d^{-1} \mathbb{P}(V \leq \floor{(n+t)/2} - n - 1) \right),
\end{align*}
so that if $t \leq n$, recalling that $\frac{1}{d+1} \leq \frac{1}{2}$ ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:4fb112986ac93341fd5cb766bc4671a00b1ac4cfa6ed5aea8463f858a5d082de","revision_digest":"sha256:d5fd7aa6284c99b0538fb1d7002f15360eed0f374a6e140643c27ddee91f3a66","source_id":"arxiv:2605.08844","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

body:
[Theorem thm:mp-mbic of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n$ be $\mathcal{S}_d^{[0,1]}$-valued independent random matrices with common mean $ \mathbf{M} $ and variance $ \mathbf{V} $. We denote by $\overline{ \mathbf{X} }_n$ their sample average and $\widehat{ \mathbf{V} }_n$ the Bessel-corrected sample variance.
    Then, for any $\alpha \in (0,1)$,
    \begin{equation} 
     \mathbb P \left( \lambda_{\max} \left( \overline \mathbf{X} _n - \mathbf{M}  \right) \geqslant   D_n^{\mathsf{meb1c}} \right) \leqslant \alpha,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}\small
    D_n^{\mathsf{meb1c}} =  \sqrt{  \frac{2 \log \frac{nd}{(n-1)\alpha}    }{n} } \left( \|\widehat \mathbf{V} _n\|^{1/2}  +  \sqrt{\frac{\log(2nd/\alpha)}{ 2 n \|\widehat{ \mathbf{V} }_n \| }} \wedge \left( \frac{2\log(2nd/\alpha)}{n} \right)^{1/4}  \right)   + \frac{ \log  \frac{nd}{(n-1)\alpha} }{3n} .
\end{equation}
Further, if $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n$ are i.i.d.,
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{n\to\infty} \sqrt{n}   D_n^{\mathsf{meb1c}} = \sqrt{2\log(d/\alpha) \| \mathbf{V} \| }, \quad \text{almost surely}.
\end{equation}

