[Theorem thm:balanced of arXiv:2511.10630, 'Cutoff for generalised Bernoulli--Laplace urn models'] Let $d \geqslant 2$ be a fixed integer. Consider a sequence $\{\mathbf{X}^{(n)}\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of balanced generalised Bernoulli--Laplace chains where the $n^{\mathrm{th}}$ chain has parameters $(d, n, \mu_n)$. Suppose that the Cheeger constant of the single ball transition matrix satisfies $\Phi_{\ast}^{(n)} = \Omega(1)$. Then, for any $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$, we have
    \begin{equation}

        \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{t^{(n)}_{\textup{mix}}(\varepsilon)}{n t_{\textup{mix,\,single}}^{(n)}(1/\sqrt{n})} = 1,
    \end{equation}
    where $t_{\textup{mix,\,single}}^{(n)}(1/\sqrt{n})$ denotes the $(1/\sqrt{n})$-mixing time of the rate $1$ single ball chain. In particular, the sequence $\{\mathbf{X}^{(n)}\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ exhibits cutoff. Moreover, the cutoff window is of size $O(n)$. Further, if $\mu_n$ is symmetric for each $n \in \mathbb{N}$, then the equality in (equation eqn:mix2 of the paper) continues to hold under the assumption that $\Phi_{\ast}^{(n)} = \Omega(n^{\alpha-1})$ for some $\alpha > 0$, with a cutoff window of size $O(n/\gamma_n)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary 30 (Careless logarithmic scale) of arXiv:2605.14511, 'Clumsy and Careless:\\ Stationary-Entry Flux in Non-monotone Coupon Collectors'] For fixed $p\in(0,1)$ and $q=1-p$,
\[
        \log T_{n,p}
        =\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\log\frac1q
          +\log\frac{n^n}{n!}
          +\OO_{\mathbb P}(1).
\]
Equivalently, by Stirling's formula,
\[
        \log T_{n,p}
        =\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\log\frac1{1-p}
          +n-\frac12\log(2\pi n)+\OO_{\mathbb P}(1).
\]

[Lemma of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] If $f\randin \mathcal{C}^r(M)$ is a Gaussian field, then its covariance function $C$ is in $\mathcal{C}^{r,r}(M\times M)$ and for any $l,k\le r$, we have
    \begin{equation} 
\mathrm{Cov}\left( j^l_xf, j^k_y f\right)=\mathbb{E}\left\{ j^l_xf \otimes j^k_y f \right\}=j^{l,k}_{x,y} C \in J^{l,k}_{x,y}M,
    \end{equation}
    and the analogous identity holds for $\nabla^{a,b}_{x,y}C$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition $\mathcal{S}$-condition of arXiv:2509.20779, 'Diffusive Scaling limit of stochastic Box-Ball systems and P\MakeLowercase{ush}TASEP'] Let $S$ be the convex polyhedron in (equation eq:def_polyhedron of the paper). We say a $d \times m$ matrix $R$ satisfies the \textit{$\mathcal S$-condition} with respect to  $S$ if and only if one of the following conditions, which are equivalent, holds: 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item[(a)] For each maximum subset 
		$I \subseteq \{1,2,\dots, m\}$, the associated principal matrix $(NR)_I$ of $NR$ has a vector $\lambda_I>\mathbf 0$ such that $(NR)_I\lambda_I>\mathbf 0$.
		\item[(b)] For each maximum subset $I \subseteq \{1,2,\dots, m\}$, the associated principal matrix $(NR)_I$ of $NR$ has a vector $\lambda_I>\mathbf 0$ such that $\lambda^\top_I(NR)_I>\mathbf 0$.
	\end{itemize}

[Thm T loc dim of arXiv:2608.25471, 'Exact Hausdorff measures and fine geometry of collisions of independent Markov processes'] Almost surely under $\hat{P}_{\bm{\rho}}$, for every $t \in (0,\infty)$,
  \begin{equation}
    \lim_{\delta\downarrow0}
    \frac{\log\mathcal{C}_t([s-\delta,s+\delta])}{\log\delta}
    =
    d_{\mathrm{ct}},
    \qquad
    \mathcal{C}_t\text{-}\mathrm{a.e.}\ s.
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem 20 of arXiv:2604.06122, 'REM universality for linear random energy'] Given $a\in (0,\varsigma)$ and a $h-$measurable sequence $(A_n)_{n\in \N}$ such that
\been{

\lim_{n\to \infty}\res A_n(h)=a,\qquad \gas
}
we have
\been{

\lim_{n\to \infty}\Lambda_n(h,A_n(h))=\hat \lambda(a),\qquad \gas
}
and 
\been{

\lim_{n\to \infty} \res M^*_n(h,A_n(h))=G^*(a),\qquad \gas.
}

