[Definition qshprod of arXiv:2411.06827, 'The Exponential Lie Series and a Chen--Strichartz Formula for L{\'e}vy Processes'] The commutative quasi-shuffle product $\star$ on ${\mathbb R}{\langle}{\mathbb A}{\rangle}$  is  inductively defined by
\begin{align}

\begin{aligned}
	u\,& \star\, {\mathbf 1}
	= {\mathbf 1}\,\star\, u
	=u, \\
	ua \star vb & 
	=(u \star vb)a + (ua \star v)b + (u \star v)[a,b],
\end{aligned}
\end{align}
where $u$ and $v$ are words and $a$ and $b$ are letters, and where
 `$ {\mathbf 1}$' is the empty word.

[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2608.12070, 'Central limit theorems for a heavy-tailed subcritical Galton-Watson process with state-dep'] Suppose the process $\mathbf{X}=\{X_n:n\geq0\}$ is defined by \eqref{111} and satisfies {\bf (C)}. Then as $n\to \infty$,  
\begin{align*}
\frac{S_n}{a_n}\stackrel{d}{\rightarrow} \pi_0^{-1/\gamma}  T,
\end{align*}
where  $\{a_n\}$ is an integer-valued sequence satisfying $na_n^{-\gamma}l_3(a_n)\to(1-m)^\gamma\Gamma(1-\gamma)^{-1}$, and $T$ is a positive random variable with Laplace transform $\mathrm{e}^{-s^\gamma}$ ($s>0$). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition def:well_posed_lin of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $s\in [0,T)$. We say that SDE$(b,\sigma;s)$ enjoys
\begin{itemize}
\item[-] \emph{\textcolor{black}{weak} existence}, if there exists a solution to SDE$(b,\sigma;s,\textcolor{black}{\nu})$ for any $\nu\in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$;
\item[-] \emph{\textcolor{black}{weak} uniqueness}, if, for any $\nu\in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, two solutions to SDE$(b,\sigma;s,\textcolor{black}{\nu})$ have the same law;
\item[-] \emph{\textcolor{black}{pathwise} existence}, if there exists a solution to SDE$(b,\sigma;s,\textcolor{black}{\xi, W, (\mathcal{F}_t)_t})$ for any $(W, (\mathcal{F}_t)_t )$ Brownian motion  and any \textcolor{black}{$\xi\in m\mathcal{F}_s$};
\item[-] \emph{\textcolor{black}{pathwise} uniqueness}, if, for any pair of general solutions $(( \mathcal{F}_t)_{t},W, X)$, $( (\mathcal{F}_t)_{t},W, X')$ to SDE$(b,\sigma;s)$ with $X_s = X'_s$ almost surely, $X = X'$ almost surely.
\end{itemize} 

We say that SDE$(b,\sigma;s)$ is weakly (or pathwise) well-posed if weak (or pathwise) existence and uniqueness hold.

Finally, we say that SDE$(b,\sigma)$ is weakly (or pathwise) well-posed if SDE$(b,\sigma;s)$ is weakly (or pathwise) well-posed for any $s\in [0,T)$.

[Thm 24 of arXiv:2606.27078, 'Semimartingale Optimal Transport with Jumps: A General Framework and Equivalent Formulatio'] Let $\mu_0,\mu_1\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ have finite second moments and let $\ell$ (defined in \eqref{definel}) satisfy Assumptions~\ref{L} and~\ref{uL}. Then 
\begin{equation*}
MBB(\mu_0,\mu_1)=MPDE(\mu_0,\mu_1),
\end{equation*}
and the infimum for the problem $MBB(\mu_0,\mu_1)$ of~\eqref{MBB} is achieved whenever it is finite. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

