[Theorem thm:global_clt of arXiv:2507.10531, 'Quantitative central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] As in (equation eq:sigmadef_global_intro of the paper), set
\begin{equation}

    \sigma_n^2 = 
    \mleft( 1 - 2 p (1-p) \sum_{j=1}^K \beta_j \mathsf{e}_j (\mathsf{e}_j-1) p^{\mathsf{e}_j-2} \mright)^{-1}
    \times p(1-p) \binom{n}{2},
\end{equation}
Then for any $\varepsilon > 0$, if $X$ is distributed as (equation eq:tilt of the paper) with $Y \sim \mathcal{G}(n,p)$ and $\mathsf{g}$ given in
(equation eq:smallgdef of the paper), under the ``phase-uniqueness or forest'' assumption, we have
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{d} \mleft( \frac{\mathsf{E}(X) - \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{E}(X)]}{\sigma_n}, Z \mright) \lesssim n^{-\frac{1}{2} + \varepsilon},
\end{equation}
for both $\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{d}_\mathrm{Was}$ and $\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{d}_\mathrm{Kol}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

