{"artifact_digest":"sha256:e34388223f2dbc6f802f3fa8c53ad713037fa21f3bf01bf065ca8abd6550d5b2","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/e3/e34388223f2dbc6f802f3fa8c53ad713037fa21f3bf01bf065ca8abd6550d5b2.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:dd1b3177c1a731546cfdba60e3272688a27f6dcd213669bef6074a253621c2fc","bundle_entry_key":"2609-00970-theorem-5-k-ball","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_073","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_073.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-00970-proposition-5-k-ball","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Suppose that $B$ has center $p\in M$ and radius $r>0$. Define the following functions for $x\in M$:
$$
g(x) = \min\left\{{r,d(x,p)}\right\} \qquad\text{and}\qquad h(x) = g(x)-g(0) .
$$
Then $h\in B_{\Lip_0(M)}$, $h(x)-h(p)=d(x,p)$ for $x\in B$, and $h$ is constant outside of $B$. We claim that
\begin{equation}

B_{\mathcal{K}({B})} = B_{\Lip_0(M)} \cap (B_{\Lip_0(M)}+h) \cap (B_{\Lip_0(M)}-h) .
\end{equation}
This will be enough to prove the proposition. Indeed, $B_{\Lip_0(M)}$ and its translates are weak$^*$ compact with respect to any predual, so the same holds for $B_{\mathcal{K}({B})}$, and the Krein-\v{S}mulyan theorem implies that $\mathcal{K}({B})$ is weak$^*$ closed.

