{"artifact_digest":"sha256:85a8e77c84b553a8064f0b3b621d0e0aa0b102e1e5ac8a90bb58517fe522243a","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/85/85a8e77c84b553a8064f0b3b621d0e0aa0b102e1e5ac8a90bb58517fe522243a.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:93a49fbc7c7ec719e54e0f8367135b51ccfa4faf0997f634efdabdb4e07cc572","bundle_entry_key":"2609-03736-lemma-41-chebyshev","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_163","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_163.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-03736-lemma-41-chebyshev","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $S_N^2=I-D_N^2$.  Since
$\Lambda_N=S_N^2+\xi_N\xi_N^{\mathsf T}$, Sherman--Morrison gives
\begin{equation}
 \xi_N^{\mathsf T}(\Lambda_N-zI)^{-1}\xi_N
 =\frac{h_N(z)}{1+h_N(z)},
 \qquad h_N(z)=\xi_N^{\mathsf T}(S_N^2-zI)^{-1}\xi_N.

\end{equation}
Because $S_N^2$ is diagonal and
$\xi_N=N^{-1/2}(\eta_{N,0},\ldots,\eta_{N,N-1})^{\mathsf T}$,
\begin{equation}
 h_N(z)=\frac1N\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}
 \frac{\eta_{N,j}^2}{1-\eta_{N,j}^2-z}.

\end{equation}
Put $x^2=1-z$.  The roots of $T_N$ are precisely the $\eta_{N,j}$, and
they are invariant as a set under $\eta\mapsto-\eta$.  Hence the
logarithmic derivative of $T_N$ gives
\begin{align}
 \sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\frac1{x^2-\eta_{N,j}^2}
