[Definition 5 of arXiv:2609.01098, 'Gaussian upper bounds for averaged heat semigroups on graphs'] The {\em relative Faber-Krahn inequality} $FK_a(n,r_1,r_2)$ holds in $B$, if for all $x\in B$, $r\in[r_1,r_2]$ and $U\subset B_x(r) $, we have
\begin{equation*}
\lambda(U):=\inf_{u\in\cC_c(U),\|u\|_2= 1}\cE(u)\geq \frac{a}{r^2}\left(\frac{m(B_x(r))}{m(U)}\right)^\frac{2}{n}.
\end{equation*}

[Lemma 3 (Frenet transition for the conserved-vector ansatz) of arXiv:2609.00673, '2609.00673'] For arbitrary real coefficients $y_i$ and $x_i=\tan(\theta_i/2)$, set
\[
Q_i=T_i-x_iN_i+y_iB_i,\qquad 1\le i\le n-1.
\]
For every $1\le i\le n-2$, the Frenet transition gives
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
Q_{i+1}=T_i
&+\bigl(x_{i+1}\cos\phi_i-y_{i+1}\sin\phi_i\bigr)N_i\\
&+\bigl(x_{i+1}\sin\phi_i+y_{i+1}\cos\phi_i\bigr)B_i.
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}
Hence $Q_{i+1}=Q_i$ if and only if
\begin{align}
-x_i&=x_{i+1}\cos\phi_i-y_{i+1}\sin\phi_i,\\
y_i&=x_{i+1}\sin\phi_i+y_{i+1}\cos\phi_i.
\end{align}
No division by $\sin\phi_i$ is used in these identities, so they remain valid for $\phi_i=\pi$.

